@@ -766,3 +766,7 @@
 `historical_sources()` enumerates the existing bounded source directory and derives retained-domain/retired-page observations from current windows, without a second catalog. These are observations, never checkpoint permissions. Recovery/source metadata is process-local; persisted checkpoints, archive aliases and system-wide ForgetSource need their own fences. Full source staging and branch copies have real costs; recovery quota is not an RSS or model-workspace upper bound.
 
 Program publishes the current source receipt after prefill and accepted-output units. Engine copies that receipt into the owning RequestRecord and terminal GenerationResult; it never derives it from the global last-source gauge. This also preserves a partial cancelled input's receipt. The bounded directory reports explicit branch parent/prefix metadata, so clients can distinguish source branches from later independent requests without a second catalog.
+
+`Engine::forget_historical_source` revokes one source and its explicit descendant family within the current RAM Engine. Engine excludes submissions with the queue lock while holding its execution lock, and refuses any outstanding request, including a terminal handle whose result has not been consumed. Program preflights the current ancestor receipt and native owners, constructs any empty window replacements, then discards related native history/checkpoint/coverage aliases before erasing source records. No active device continuation is released behind ResourceManager's back. Family membership follows existing source identities without allocating a second lineage catalog; member revisions cannot hide an older derived binding.
+
+Already prepared rebuilds retain their real R charge until their owner drops them; missing source identity/revision rejects them during normal admission before native execution. Unrelated sources and native histories remain valid. The result counts forgotten source records and discarded native domains, not physical bytes released. Idle checkpoint pool buffers may still be allocated, and caller-owned IDs or copied transcripts cannot be revoked or securely erased. This process-local operation does not cover persisted files, another Engine or independent requests containing the same text; durable/global forget still requires its own archive fences. No CLI/HTTP forget control is exposed.
